1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:05,380
The previous episode found the rotation
difference from the starting to the target

2
00:00:05,660 --> 00:00:08,700
orientation. We now construct intermediate
orientations along that rotation.

3
00:00:09,350 --> 00:00:13,490
How do we construct natural intermediate
orientations between two poses?

4
00:00:13,890 --> 00:00:16,230
We will observe driving and flight,

5
00:00:16,230 --> 00:00:20,150
then visualize the short path and speed of
quaternion interpolation.

6
00:00:20,390 --> 00:00:23,910
Next, we will convert Euler angles,
axis-angle,

7
00:00:23,910 --> 00:00:26,030
quaternions and matrices under one convention.

8
00:00:26,370 --> 00:00:29,350
Finally, we will calculate the midpoint of a

9
00:00:29,350 --> 00:00:32,450
ninety-degree turn and verify a conversion
round trip.

10
00:00:33,100 --> 00:00:36,580
This is driving on a bend in Forza Horizon4.

11
00:00:36,900 --> 00:00:39,820
Read the car facing separately from the
following camera.

12
00:00:43,148 --> 00:00:45,748
Matching the first and last directions does
not

13
00:00:45,748 --> 00:00:47,848
prevent an awkward sudden turn between them.

14
00:00:51,016 --> 00:00:55,096
Compare the front of the car, the bend and the
moving background.

15
00:00:58,364 --> 00:01:01,764
The footage does not reveal which
interpolation function the game uses.

16
00:01:02,184 --> 00:01:04,904
It shows why intermediate orientations matter.

17
00:01:08,192 --> 00:01:12,252
Changes in apparent car size also involve
camera and distance.

18
00:01:12,332 --> 00:01:18,212
Following its facing separately helps avoid
mixing translation with rotation.

19
00:01:21,340 --> 00:01:25,460
Our next diagram will hold position and camera
fixed and change only orientation.

20
00:01:26,200 --> 00:01:28,140
After observing the body changing direction,

21
00:01:28,500 --> 00:01:31,860
can we make intermediate poses by mixing the
four numbers at the start and end?

22
00:01:32,770 --> 00:01:37,410
Consider the midpoint between identity and
a180-degree turn about z.

23
00:01:37,750 --> 00:01:43,150
Averaging the entries yields a diagonal
of0,0,1. Two axes collapse.

24
00:01:43,370 --> 00:01:45,850
Quaternions must maintain unit length too.

25
00:01:45,957 --> 00:01:49,530
A component blend generally leaves the unit
surface.

26
00:01:49,770 --> 00:01:55,330
We will distinguish a normalized linear blend
from following an arc on the unit sphere.

27
00:01:55,610 --> 00:01:58,210
Matrices can be interpolated with methods that
respect the rotation structure;

28
00:01:58,250 --> 00:02:01,170
naive entry blending is the problem.

29
00:02:01,817 --> 00:02:06,217
As we learned, q and minus q represent the
same orientation,

30
00:02:06,537 --> 00:02:08,897
yet a component path between them can differ.

31
00:02:09,237 --> 00:02:12,577
Take the dot product of unit endpoints. If it
is negative,

32
00:02:12,857 --> 00:02:15,777
flip every component of the end quaternion.

33
00:02:16,057 --> 00:02:21,597
This selects the closer representative of the
same endpoint; it is not conjugation.

34
00:02:21,857 --> 00:02:25,977
For endpoints q and minus q,

35
00:02:26,297 --> 00:02:28,857
sign alignment makes them identical, avoiding
a zero midpoint.

36
00:02:28,857 --> 00:02:33,877
A physical180-degree separation has two
equally short paths.

37
00:02:34,197 --> 00:02:36,557
Choose a direction with an explicit policy.

38
00:02:36,757 --> 00:02:39,037
We can also use the relative rotation from the
previous lesson.

39
00:02:39,357 --> 00:02:43,037
After sign alignment, delta is the end
quaternion times the inverse of the start.

40
00:02:43,337 --> 00:02:47,237
Applying delta to the power t to the start
follows the same short arc.

41
00:02:47,867 --> 00:02:50,567
In delta to the power t, t is the exponent on
delta;

42
00:02:50,567 --> 00:02:56,147
the expression does not square t.

43
00:02:56,507 --> 00:02:59,987
With a z ninety-degree delta and t equal to
one half,

44
00:02:59,987 --> 00:03:03,767
apply half the difference, forty-five degrees,
to the starting orientation.

45
00:03:03,967 --> 00:03:09,527
We will now calculate weights that keep the
intermediate orientation on the unit arc.

46
00:03:10,150 --> 00:03:15,414
Observe connected poses in Skate 3. Red
follows the body direction.

47
00:03:16,058 --> 00:03:18,878
Blue follows the visible board length. Follow
takeoff,

48
00:03:19,362 --> 00:03:21,622
the airborne pose and landing separately.

49
00:03:22,226 --> 00:03:24,426
The fraction for constructing intermediate
poses is t.

50
00:03:24,850 --> 00:03:29,530
The previous forty-five degrees was a worked
example, not an angle measured here.

51
00:03:30,150 --> 00:03:34,910
Track the car facing as it follows the next
bend.

52
00:03:37,762 --> 00:03:40,442
An endpoint image alone does not tell whether
the

53
00:03:40,442 --> 00:03:45,734
turn was short or included an extra
revolution.

54
00:03:45,994 --> 00:03:49,934
Interpolation between two orientations chooses
a path

55
00:03:50,294 --> 00:03:53,454
without storing the number of earlier
revolutions.

56
00:03:56,266 --> 00:04:04,278
Interpolating a position along the road
differs from interpolating orientation.

57
00:04:04,278 --> 00:04:10,798
Even when two moments have similar facing, the
intervening turn requires observing

58
00:04:11,258 --> 00:04:14,118
the motion. Distinguish endpoint orientation
from rotation history over time.

59
00:04:17,230 --> 00:04:21,110
Now we will relate an arc on the unit sphere
to the orientation change.

60
00:04:22,150 --> 00:04:26,070
Following an arc requires the angle between
its endpoints.

61
00:04:26,330 --> 00:04:28,670
Begin with the familiar unit circle in a
plane.

62
00:04:28,910 --> 00:04:31,230
The rightward unit vector is one, zero.

63
00:04:31,430 --> 00:04:34,750
A thirty-degree vector is square root three
over two, one half.

64
00:04:35,350 --> 00:04:39,330
Multiplying corresponding entries and adding
gives square root three over two.

65
00:04:39,430 --> 00:04:41,910
That number is cosine of thirty degrees.

66
00:04:42,010 --> 00:04:47,030
Arccosine performs the reverse lookup and
returns thirty degrees.

67
00:04:47,030 --> 00:04:49,750
For quaternions, the dot sums four component
products and

68
00:04:49,750 --> 00:04:52,710
gives the angle alpha in the section joining
the two unit points.

69
00:04:53,130 --> 00:04:56,210
Alpha must be distinguished from the object's
physical rotation.

70
00:04:56,967 --> 00:05:00,127
Unit quaternions lie on a sphere in four
dimensions,

71
00:05:00,132 --> 00:05:02,307
with squared components summing to one.

72
00:05:02,527 --> 00:05:05,407
This circle illustrates the relevant arc in a
two-dimensional section,

73
00:05:05,687 --> 00:05:08,767
rather than depicting the full
four-dimensional sphere.

74
00:05:08,937 --> 00:05:14,447
Let d be the sign-aligned dot product, and let
alpha be its arccosine.

75
00:05:14,627 --> 00:05:19,227
The quaternion arc angle differs from the
physical turn

76
00:05:19,487 --> 00:05:22,307
angle: the minimum physical rotation is twice
alpha.

77
00:05:22,307 --> 00:05:27,347
Sine-ratio weights combine the endpoints into
the point at fraction t of the arc.

78
00:05:27,787 --> 00:05:32,347
This is spherical linear interpolation. It
does not mean the components change linearly.

79
00:05:32,983 --> 00:05:36,543
Red follows the body; blue follows the board
direction.

80
00:05:37,443 --> 00:05:40,803
Their directions change in the air before
another landing.

81
00:05:41,883 --> 00:05:45,123
Unit length in the calculation is a condition
on the stored direction values.

82
00:05:46,143 --> 00:05:50,863
It does not set the game travel distance or
the displayed line length to one.

83
00:05:51,483 --> 00:05:55,083
We found the unit arc, but its denominator
becomes small as the endpoints coincide.

84
00:05:55,423 --> 00:05:57,943
Handle nearly identical orientations first.

85
00:05:58,567 --> 00:06:02,887
Our code requires finite t between zero and
one.

86
00:06:03,427 --> 00:06:06,027
Validate and normalize both quaternion
endpoints first.

87
00:06:06,177 --> 00:06:13,687
Near-zero or nonfinite inputs need an error or
an explicit fallback to a valid orientation.

88
00:06:13,927 --> 00:06:16,227
Flip the endpoint when the dot is negative,

89
00:06:16,227 --> 00:06:20,527
then clamp the dot to protect arccosine from
rounding error.

90
00:06:20,667 --> 00:06:23,107
When d exceeds0.9995, this example uses
normalized

91
00:06:23,367 --> 00:06:27,607
linear interpolation to avoid a small sine
denominator.

92
00:06:27,607 --> 00:06:30,427
Otherwise, use the sine weights and normalize
the result.

93
00:06:30,887 --> 00:06:33,627
Check the endpoint orientations at t zero and
one.

94
00:06:34,383 --> 00:06:38,683
In these other driving intervals, separate car
facing from background motion.

95
00:06:41,583 --> 00:06:45,463
The edit between excerpts is not a computed
intermediate pose.

96
00:06:45,663 --> 00:06:49,023
Observe continuous motion within each excerpt.

97
00:06:51,463 --> 00:06:59,023
Code produces intermediate rotations by
changing t between the endpoints.

98
00:06:59,223 --> 00:07:04,443
Imagine both a forward line and an up line on
the car.

99
00:07:04,643 --> 00:07:10,603
A single direction can miss its twist.

100
00:07:10,603 --> 00:07:14,523
A cut can change location and camera. Observe
continuous pose changes within

101
00:07:14,983 --> 00:07:20,183
each excerpt before connecting them to the
next fixed-condition calculation.

102
00:07:23,083 --> 00:07:28,343
The next calculation holds camera and position
fixed. For a ninety-degree turn,

103
00:07:28,343 --> 00:07:31,643
examine t equal to zero point two five and
zero point five.

104
00:07:32,383 --> 00:07:34,723
To verify the observation made with a moving
camera,

105
00:07:35,063 --> 00:07:39,883
fix the camera and z axis and compute the
intermediate poses of a ninety-degree turn.

106
00:07:40,517 --> 00:07:43,277
Start at[1,0,0,0], ending at a z90-degree

107
00:07:43,497 --> 00:07:49,197
turn:[cos45degrees,0,0,sin45degrees].

108
00:07:49,497 --> 00:07:51,877
The dot is square root two over two: alpha
is45degrees,

109
00:07:51,997 --> 00:07:55,877
while the physical rotation is90degrees.

110
00:07:55,952 --> 00:07:59,657
At t equal to zero point two five,

111
00:08:00,157 --> 00:08:02,577
the object rotates twenty-two point five
degrees, the first of four equal steps.

112
00:08:02,777 --> 00:08:06,157
At the halfway value t equal to zero point
five, it rotates forty-five degrees.

113
00:08:06,219 --> 00:08:10,377
At the third step, t equal to zero point seven
five, it is sixty-seven point five degrees.

114
00:08:10,517 --> 00:08:13,597
Equal t intervals give equal rotation-angle
intervals.

115
00:08:13,977 --> 00:08:17,717
Each quaternion is converted to a matrix to
draw the body axes.

116
00:08:17,937 --> 00:08:20,897
We can verify intermediate results as well as
endpoints.

117
00:08:21,657 --> 00:08:25,157
NLERP aligns signs, blends components linearly
and

118
00:08:25,297 --> 00:08:27,617
normalizes: a simple calculation that
preserves unit length.

119
00:08:27,937 --> 00:08:32,237
Linear progression of t generally does not
produce constant physical angular speed.

120
00:08:32,417 --> 00:08:34,397
Between identity and a120-degree turn,

121
00:08:34,410 --> 00:08:37,817
NLERP at one quarter rotates
approximately27.8degrees.

122
00:08:37,852 --> 00:08:40,357
SLERP gives30degrees there. Both give60degrees
halfway,

123
00:08:40,557 --> 00:08:44,537
but differ at other points along the path.

124
00:08:44,777 --> 00:08:48,897
Small differences may be hard to see.

125
00:08:49,042 --> 00:08:51,817
Compare motion requirements and cost without
assuming SLERP is always faster.

126
00:08:52,450 --> 00:08:57,030
Watch the order in which the red body line and
blue board line change.

127
00:08:58,220 --> 00:09:01,980
Travel distance and the fraction between
orientations are different values.

128
00:09:03,170 --> 00:09:05,910
The camera also follows the action.

129
00:09:07,020 --> 00:09:11,460
Fast-looking screen motion does not establish
constant angular speed.

130
00:09:12,670 --> 00:09:15,430
We will state the timing conditions separately
in the calculation.

131
00:09:16,050 --> 00:09:21,050
Driving speed and angular speed are separate
quantities.

132
00:09:22,834 --> 00:09:27,834
Observe distance travelled and change in car
facing separately.

133
00:09:29,638 --> 00:09:34,758
The camera also moves, so this footage is not
a measurement of exact angular velocity.

134
00:09:35,058 --> 00:09:39,102
The speedometer reports travel speed, not the
angular velocity of orientation.

135
00:09:39,102 --> 00:09:46,766
Keep units and measured quantities distinct.

136
00:09:47,086 --> 00:09:51,006
A large camera turn does not imply the same
car rotation.

137
00:09:51,006 --> 00:09:56,870
Observe car and road together to separate pose
changes.

138
00:09:57,110 --> 00:10:01,670
Our fixed-axis calculation can compare turn
angles over equal time intervals.

139
00:10:01,950 --> 00:10:04,430
Changing the time function changes the speed.

140
00:10:05,050 --> 00:10:06,870
Using intermediate orientations in playback

141
00:10:06,870 --> 00:10:10,310
also requires deciding how t increases over
time.

142
00:10:11,045 --> 00:10:18,113
Constant angular speed requires fixed
endpoints and t that is linear in time.

143
00:10:18,393 --> 00:10:24,113
An easing function follows the same arc with a
varying speed.

144
00:10:24,245 --> 00:10:27,253
Repeatedly interpolating from the current pose
toward a

145
00:10:27,253 --> 00:10:31,033
target differs from one fixed-endpoint
interpolation.

146
00:10:31,333 --> 00:10:35,353
A fixed fraction per frame may yield different
responses at different frame rates.

147
00:10:35,713 --> 00:10:38,033
Design the fraction using elapsed time.

148
00:10:38,033 --> 00:10:42,373
Moving-target tracking and long animation
curves require their own design;

149
00:10:42,813 --> 00:10:45,053
a single SLERP does not solve everything.

150
00:10:45,867 --> 00:10:49,787
After the time conditions, compare the same
rotation with small numbers. This defined

151
00:10:50,067 --> 00:10:55,787
example fixes the camera and z axis and turns
from zero to ninety degrees over one second.

152
00:10:56,107 --> 00:11:02,207
At half a second, elapsed time divided by
duration gives t equal to one half.

153
00:11:02,467 --> 00:11:06,107
With linear time, half of ninety degrees is
forty-five degrees.

154
00:11:06,327 --> 00:11:09,507
Now use the square of the elapsed-time
fraction as t.

155
00:11:09,507 --> 00:11:11,747
One half squared is one quarter, so the same
instant gives one

156
00:11:11,747 --> 00:11:16,147
quarter of ninety degrees: twenty-two point
five degrees.

157
00:11:16,507 --> 00:11:20,747
At one second both reach t equal to one and
ninety degrees.

158
00:11:21,047 --> 00:11:26,047
The endpoints and arc stay the same, but
progress at intermediate times differs.

159
00:11:26,327 --> 00:11:30,607
Choosing the path and mapping time to progress
are separate decisions.

160
00:11:31,233 --> 00:11:32,873
Even after choosing a timing rule, we must
check whether the

161
00:11:32,873 --> 00:11:37,053
final orientation retains the record of an
extra complete turn.

162
00:11:38,023 --> 00:11:40,603
Making w positive independently each frame can
create

163
00:11:40,603 --> 00:11:43,203
a component sign jump at the180-degree
boundary.

164
00:11:43,443 --> 00:11:46,763
Flipping a new quaternion when its dot with
the previous

165
00:11:46,763 --> 00:11:49,983
one is negative can maintain a nearby
representative.

166
00:11:50,323 --> 00:11:54,963
That choice helps component continuity, but
does not recover the number of revolutions.

167
00:11:55,243 --> 00:11:57,923
If an animation intentionally spins a full
turn,

168
00:11:57,923 --> 00:12:00,603
preserve intermediate keys or accumulated turn
information.

169
00:12:00,823 --> 00:12:04,183
The desired direction at180degrees is a design

170
00:12:04,283 --> 00:12:07,663
policy: shortest alone does not make the
choice unique.

171
00:12:08,283 --> 00:12:11,043
The red body direction changes and becomes
upright again.

172
00:12:12,038 --> 00:12:15,238
The current pose and the rotation history are
different information.

173
00:12:16,333 --> 00:12:20,695
This is a different excerpt. Follow the blue
board direction separately as well.

174
00:12:21,497 --> 00:12:25,697
Passing the final orientation does not also
store how many turns occurred.

175
00:12:26,317 --> 00:12:30,217
This is wingsuit flight down a mountain in
Riders Republic.

176
00:12:30,357 --> 00:12:35,770
Observe the bank of the arms and body.

177
00:12:35,950 --> 00:12:40,370
Position continues changing while facing and
up directions change too.

178
00:12:40,630 --> 00:12:47,783
One facing arrow does not capture the whole
orientation.

179
00:12:48,083 --> 00:12:56,157
Storing the arrival orientation does not
preserve every rotation made along the flight.

180
00:12:56,497 --> 00:12:59,157
Separate the following camera motion too.

181
00:12:59,357 --> 00:13:03,057
This footage does not expose internal
quaternion or interpolation code.

182
00:13:03,057 --> 00:13:11,270
With different equipment too, distinguish
torso facing from body bank.

183
00:13:11,450 --> 00:13:14,970
Attached forward and up directions provide a
frame for reading orientation.

184
00:13:15,210 --> 00:13:22,583
Do not connect cuts or rewind screens into
continuous motion. We selected actual

185
00:13:22,783 --> 00:13:31,037
action within separate excerpts; imagine the
three attached axes in each.

186
00:13:31,377 --> 00:13:35,697
We will now choose a suitable representation
for each task and convert between them.

187
00:13:36,317 --> 00:13:41,217
We now have an intermediate orientation at one
instant.

188
00:13:41,217 --> 00:13:45,437
Editing its angles and applying it to many
vertices call for different representations.

189
00:13:45,897 --> 00:13:50,097
Keep the same body axes while passing that
orientation between editable angles,

190
00:13:50,097 --> 00:13:52,977
interpolation quaternions and matrices that
transform vectors.

191
00:13:53,600 --> 00:13:56,900
Euler angles suit human reading and editing,
with order,

192
00:13:57,080 --> 00:14:00,600
equivalent encodings and singular poses to
handle.

193
00:14:00,840 --> 00:14:03,140
Quaternions suit composition and
interpolation,

194
00:14:03,215 --> 00:14:07,360
but need unit-length and sign management and
are hard to edit directly.

195
00:14:07,620 --> 00:14:12,120
Matrices expose the three axes and suit
transforming many vectors.

196
00:14:12,380 --> 00:14:14,740
Check orthonormality and determinant.

197
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:19,720
Float32 components alone occupy12bytes for
Euler,16for a

198
00:14:19,720 --> 00:14:24,720
quaternion and36for a3-by-3matrix, excluding
padding and metadata.

199
00:14:25,220 --> 00:14:28,060
Axis-angle explains a single turn clearly.
Measure compression and runtime in your

200
00:14:28,380 --> 00:14:34,620
implementation; other representations also
have rotation-aware interpolation methods.

201
00:14:34,940 --> 00:14:37,580
Storage or transmission may compress
components.

202
00:14:38,140 --> 00:14:42,920
Reconstructing an omitted component from unit
length requires sign rules

203
00:14:43,025 --> 00:14:46,160
and quantization-error checks. Sixteen bytes
is not a universal minimum.

204
00:14:47,083 --> 00:14:49,703
Between two orientations, we chose a path,
kept unit length and specified

205
00:14:49,703 --> 00:14:55,683
how t follows physical time. Extra turn
history needed separate storage.

206
00:14:56,003 --> 00:14:59,123
We now pass one intermediate orientation to
the next task.

207
00:14:59,403 --> 00:15:03,623
The next episode retains the same object and
axes while converting angles,

208
00:15:03,623 --> 00:15:06,703
quaternions and matrices and verifying the
same rotation.
